<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  lang="en"
  dir="ltr"
  hreflang={hreflang}
  schema={schema}
  lastmod="2026-10-12"
>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} lang="en" />
        <h1 class="hero__h1">Civil Marriage in Abu Dhabi: Requirements, Steps and Fees</h1>
        <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated October 2026</p>
        <p class="hero__sub">
          Civil marriage in Abu Dhabi is a short court wedding at the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department&rsquo;s
          Civil Family Court, open to residents and tourists aged 18 or over, with no witnesses, guardian or
          medical test. You apply online, pay AED 300 (or AED 2,500 for the one-day service) and sign before
          a court officer. The same court hears civil divorces, so the way you marry also sets the rules if
          the marriage ever ends.
        </p>
        <ul class="hero__trust">
          <li>✓ Built on Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021</li>
          <li>✓ Fees from the ADJD service page</li>
          <li>✓ Civil vs Muslim marriage table</li>
          <li>✓ What it means for a later divorce</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__form">
        <LeadForm lang="en" variant="hero" heading="Prenup or Divorce Question? Ask Free" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="container main-content">

    <!-- Short answer -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
      <h2 id="answer-heading">Civil Marriage in Abu Dhabi: The Short Answer</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, you can marry in Abu Dhabi in a civil ceremony with no religious formalities, whether you live
        in the UAE or are visiting. The rules are in Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 on Civil Marriage and its
        Effects and in ADJD Chairman Decision No. 8 of 2022 (the &ldquo;2022 Regulation&rdquo;). The
        marriage is concluded at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, formerly the Non-Muslim Family Court.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Who.</strong> Foreigners of any religion and non-Muslim UAE nationals. UAE citizens who are Muslim cannot use it.</li>
        <li><strong>Age.</strong> Both spouses at least 18 (Article 4 of the Law, Article 8 of the 2022 Regulation).</li>
        <li><strong>No extras.</strong> No witnesses and no pre-marriage medical certificate (Article 5 of the Law, Article 8 of the Regulation), and a guardian is not among the conditions.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost.</strong> AED 300 for the standard service or AED 2,500 for the urgent one, according to the ADJD civil marriage service page.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">18</span>
          <span class="stat-label">minimum age for both spouses in Abu Dhabi</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">AED 300</span>
          <span class="stat-label">standard civil marriage fee</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">0</span>
          <span class="stat-label">witnesses required</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">16</span>
          <span class="stat-label">age until which joint custody runs after a civil divorce</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>Why a divorce site explains marriage</h3>
        <p>
          DivorceU.AE is a divorce information and lawyer-referral service, not a wedding planner or a law
          firm, and it earns when readers ask to be put in touch with a family lawyer. We cover civil marriage
          because the court that marries you would also hear a divorce, and the marriage you choose decides
          which law governs custody and money later. Most couples never need that part.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Meaning -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="meaning-heading">
      <h2 id="meaning-heading">What Does Civil Marriage Mean in Abu Dhabi?</h2>
      <p>
        A civil marriage is a marriage concluded and registered under the law without regard to any
        religious law (Article 7 of the 2022 Regulation). Article 1 of the 2021 Law defines it as the
        marriage of a man and a woman, on a permanent basis, governed by that Law. The couple consents
        before a court officer and the court issues a bilingual Arabic and English certificate, recorded in
        the ADJD&rsquo;s electronic civil marriage registry (Article 9(4) of the Regulation).
      </p>
      <p>
        It is a full legal marriage, not a symbolic ceremony; any religious or cultural celebration is
        separate. The ADJD&rsquo;s 2023 guide to the law says the vows are read in a pre-recorded voice-over
        available in seven languages, and couples may exchange rings and their own vows. According to ADJD
        figures reported by Gulf News in January 2026, the court registered about 19,000 civil marriage
        contracts in 2025 and around 53,000 since the law took effect.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Eligibility -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="eligibility-heading">
      <h2 id="eligibility-heading">Who Can Have a Civil Marriage in Abu Dhabi?</h2>
      <p>
        Any man and woman aged 18 or over who are free to marry can have a civil marriage in Abu Dhabi,
        unless one of them is a Muslim UAE national. Article 8 of the 2022 Regulation lists the conditions,
        and the ADJD Chairman may add others.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Condition</th><th>What it means</th><th>Source</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Age 18 or over</td><td>Both spouses; age proven by an official document from your country of nationality</td><td>Law Art. 4(1); Regulation Art. 8(1)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Free consent</td><td>Each of you states it explicitly, and nothing may legally prevent it</td><td>Law Art. 4(2); Regulation Art. 8(2)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mental capacity</td><td>Both spouses need the capacity to marry</td><td>Regulation Art. 8(3)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>No close blood relationship</td><td>Not between siblings, children, grandchildren, uncles or aunts</td><td>Regulation Art. 8(4)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Not already married</td><td>Neither of you may be bound by an existing marriage</td><td>Regulation Art. 8(5)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Declaration and disclosure</td><td>Both sign the form, list any previous marriage with its divorce date and confirm no other marriage exists</td><td>Law Arts. 4(3) and 5(3); Regulation Art. 9(1)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Residents and tourists</h3>
      <p>
        You do not need to live in Abu Dhabi, or anywhere in the UAE. Article 2 of the Regulation tells the
        court&rsquo;s attestation officer to conclude and register civil marriages &ldquo;for visitors and
        residents in the UAE&rdquo;, and the ADJD&rsquo;s Civil Family Court page says you can apply from
        anywhere in the world through its online portal. Video ceremonies, though, are only for couples
        where all parties are UAE residents, so visitors travel to Abu Dhabi to sign.
      </p>

      <h3>Muslims and UAE nationals</h3>
      <p>
        The ADJD&rsquo;s service pages and its 2023 question-and-answer guide say civil marriage is
        available regardless of religion or nationality, with one exclusion: UAE citizens who are Muslim. A
        Muslim who is not an Emirati can therefore apply. Non-Muslim UAE nationals are within the 2021 Law
        (Article 1), and the ADJD civil marriage page says they are exempt from the nationality restriction,
        but other ADJD and UAE Government portal pages say neither party may be a UAE national. If one of
        you is Emirati, confirm with the court before you pay.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Documents -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="documents-heading">
      <h2 id="documents-heading">Requirements for Marriage at the Abu Dhabi Court: Documents Checklist</h2>
      <p>
        For a civil marriage at the Abu Dhabi court you need the bilingual application form, each
        partner&rsquo;s passport, an Emirates ID if you have one, and proof that any earlier marriage has
        ended. That is the full list on the ADJD civil marriage page.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Item</th><th>Who needs it</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Civil marriage application form (Arabic and English)</td><td>Both, completed together</td><td>Includes the disclosure of any previous marriage (Regulation Art. 9(1)); available on the ADJD website</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Passport</td><td>Each partner</td><td>Copy with the application; the ADJD&rsquo;s 2023 guide says to bring a hard copy of your passport or Emirates ID to the ceremony</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Emirates ID</td><td>Each UAE resident</td><td>Not needed by visitors</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Divorce judgment or certificate</td><td>Anyone previously divorced</td><td>Shows the earlier marriage is dissolved</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Death certificate of a former spouse</td><td>Anyone widowed</td><td>Shows the earlier marriage ended</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Prenuptial agreement</td><td>Optional</td><td>Separate AED 950 notarisation, in Arabic and English</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pre-marriage medical certificate</td><td>Not required</td><td>Law Art. 5(1); Regulation Art. 8</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Witnesses</td><td>Not required</td><td>Regulation Art. 8; the notary officer acts as the witness</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Source: ADJD Civil Marriage and Civil Family Court pages, read 12 October 2026. Requirements can
          change, so check the service page before you apply.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Foreign divorce decrees and death certificates</h3>
      <p>
        If your proof of divorce or a former spouse&rsquo;s death certificate was issued abroad, plan for
        legalisation early. The UAE is not a party to the Hague Apostille Convention, so an apostille on its
        own is not how foreign documents are accepted here. The usual chain is certification by the issuing
        country&rsquo;s foreign ministry, then attestation by the UAE embassy or consulate in that country,
        then attestation by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MOFA) once the document is in the UAE. The
        ADJD pages list proof of divorce without saying whether the court insists on that full chain for a
        civil marriage, so ask the Civil Family Court before you start.
      </p>
      <p>
        Language is less of a hurdle here: Article 44 of the Regulation exempts
        expatriates from translating documents from English into Arabic, and the department&rsquo;s own
        translation section can translate other documents for a service fee.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Steps -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="steps-heading">
      <h2 id="steps-heading">How to Get Married in Abu Dhabi: Step by Step</h2>
      <p>
        You apply, the court checks the file, you pay, the court gives you a date and you sign at the
        ceremony. Article 9 of the 2022 Regulation sets out the legal steps.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Choose standard or urgent service</h3>
          <p>Standard costs AED 300 and the ADJD quotes up to 10 working days. Urgent costs AED 2,500, is handled within one working day and lets you pick the ceremony date and time, subject to the court&rsquo;s availability.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Submit the application</h3>
          <p>Apply through the ADJD website (with separate routes for UAE residents and tourists), an approved typing centre or an approved law firm. Complete the bilingual form, including the disclosure, and attach your documents.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>The court reviews the file</h3>
          <p>The court checks that every condition is met (Article 9(2)). The ADJD says the section either approves the application or rejects it with reasons.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Pay the fee</h3>
          <p>The current service page says you pay once the application is approved. Fees are refundable only in very limited circumstances, so check names and dates before paying.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Receive your ceremony date</h3>
          <p>The court confirms the date and time by email. Article 9(3) says the court contacts an approved couple within three working days of submission to fix a date, though the service page&rsquo;s timings are the practical guide.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Attend the ceremony</h3>
          <p>Both of you attend at the Civil Family Court in the ADJD main court building next to Zayed Sports City, or by video if all parties are UAE residents.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Sign and receive the certificate</h3>
          <p>The marriage attestation officer certifies the marriage and issues a bilingual certificate recorded in the electronic civil marriage registry (Article 9(4)). The ADJD says you receive it after the ceremony and its digital authentication.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Fees and timing at a glance</h3>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Service</th><th>Fee</th><th>Stated timing</th><th>Source</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Standard civil marriage</td><td>AED 300</td><td>Up to 10 working days</td><td>ADJD Civil Marriage page</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Urgent (express) civil marriage</td><td>AED 2,500</td><td>Within one working day; you choose the date</td><td>ADJD Civil Marriage page</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Prenuptial agreement notarisation</td><td>AED 950</td><td>Not stated</td><td>ADJD Civil Marriage page</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MOFA attestation of the certificate (optional)</td><td>AED 150</td><td>Separate MOFA process</td><td>MOFA FAQs, personal status documents</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typing centre or law firm help</td><td>Varies</td><td>Ask before they start</td><td>Not set by the court</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Fees as published on 12 October 2026. Confirm them on the ADJD civil marriage service page before
          you pay.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>On the day</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>The Civil Family Court page lists ceremony times of Monday to Thursday, 9:00am to 1:30pm, and Friday, 9:00am to 11:00am, in 30-minute slots, with times that can change during Ramadan.</li>
        <li>The court aims to finish within 15 minutes, so arrive early with your original passport or Emirates ID.</li>
        <li>Guests and photographers are welcome.</li>
        <li>If you cannot attend, tell the court at least 24 hours ahead. Without that notice you need a new application.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Prenup -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="prenup-heading">
      <h2 id="prenup-heading">Can You Sign a Prenup With a Civil Marriage in Abu Dhabi?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. Article 5(2) of the 2021 Law lets spouses agree the terms of their marriage, which then become
        their reference for the rights of husband and wife during the marriage and after divorce. The ADJD
        notarises a prenuptial agreement for AED 950, in person or, where all parties are UAE residents, by
        video.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters most for money. Article 8 of the Law says that if the marriage contract is silent on
        alimony or other financial claims, the judge decides them at their discretion using a list of
        factors. Put the other way round, terms you have written into the contract are the starting point.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ADJD sends an approved template once you apply. Its 2023 version keeps separate the property each
        of you owned before the marriage or receives by gift or inheritance during it, splits jointly owned
        assets equally unless you both declare otherwise in writing, allows a review of the terms after ten
        years, and chooses Abu Dhabi law and courts for any dispute. A custom agreement must be legally
        translated into Arabic and English. The template itself recommends
        independent legal advice, which is sensible: a clause that looks fair at 30 can look very different
        after 15 years and two children.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also fill in a will registration form when you sign the marriage contract (Article 13(2)).
        That is worth considering because, without a will, Article 11 gives half of an estate in the UAE to
        the surviving spouse and divides the other half equally between the children. What a UAE prenup can
        and cannot protect is covered in our guide to
        <a href="/en/prenuptial-agreement-uae/">prenuptial agreements in the UAE</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Certificate -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="certificate-heading">
      <h2 id="certificate-heading">Marriage Certificate in Abu Dhabi: Attestation and Recognition Abroad</h2>
      <p>
        Your marriage certificate comes from the court on the day: bilingual, digitally authenticated and
        recorded in the ADJD civil marriage registry. The UAE Government portal says there is no further
        action to take once it is issued.
      </p>
      <p>
        To use it abroad, have it attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs first; both the ADJD and
        the UAE Government portal recommend this. MOFA&rsquo;s FAQs put the attestation fee for personal
        status documents at AED 150 and say the service accepts digital documents carrying a QR code.
        Because the UAE is outside the Apostille Convention, many countries also want the document
        legalised by their own embassy or consulate in the UAE before a registry at home will accept it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recognition is decided by the other country, not by the ADJD, whose FAQ advises asking your
        embassy. Before the wedding, ask it three things: whether it recognises a UAE civil marriage, which
        attestations it wants, and whether you must register the marriage at home by a deadline. The same
        document chain applies if a marriage ends; see
        <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">divorce certificate attestation</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Federal -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="federal-heading">
      <h2 id="federal-heading">Civil Marriage in the UAE Outside Abu Dhabi</h2>
      <p>
        Outside Abu Dhabi&rsquo;s own system, civil marriage for non-Muslims runs under Federal Decree-Law
        No. 41 of 2022, in force since 1 February 2023, and the best-known service is Dubai Courts&rsquo;.
        Its rules are stricter on age, religion and residence.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Point</th><th>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</th><th>Dubai Courts (federal civil law)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Minimum age</td><td>18</td><td>21 (Article 5 of the 2022 federal law)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Religion</td><td>Any, except Muslim UAE nationals</td><td>Both partners must be non-Muslims</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Residence</td><td>None needed; tourists can apply</td><td>At least one partner resident in Dubai</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Attendance</td><td>In person, or by video if all parties are UAE residents</td><td>The couple or their legally authorised representatives in person</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Default custody after divorce</td><td>Joint until 16, then the child chooses</td><td>Joint until 18, then the child chooses (Article 4)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Dubai conditions as listed on the UAE Government portal&rsquo;s civil marriage page, read
          12 October 2026.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The portal&rsquo;s summary of the federal conditions adds legal capacity, explicit consent, a bar on
        marriage between close relatives and a rule that the husband discloses any existing marriage. Where
        you later divorce depends on where you live and work, which our
        <a href="/en/dubai-vs-abu-dhabi-divorce/">Dubai vs Abu Dhabi divorce comparison</a> explains.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Muslim marriage -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="muslim-heading">
      <h2 id="muslim-heading">Muslim Marriage at the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department</h2>
      <p>
        A Muslim marriage in Abu Dhabi is a different contract under a different law: Federal Decree-Law
        No. 41 of 2024, the Personal Status Law in force since 15 April 2025. It is also documented through
        the ADJD, but none of the civil rules above apply. In short:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Offer and acceptance, plus the guardian if the wife is Muslim. A non-citizen Muslim wife whose national law does not require a guardian is exempt (Articles 18 and 26).</li>
        <li>Both spouses 18 or over. Documenting a contract for anyone younger needs a court permit, as does a marriage to a virgin fianc&eacute;e where the age gap is over 30 years (Article 19).</li>
        <li>The bride&rsquo;s consent, with proof of it in the contract (Article 21(4)).</li>
        <li>Two witnesses: sane adult men who heard and understood the offer and acceptance, Muslim if the husband is Muslim (Articles 26 and 27).</li>
        <li>A dowry, which belongs to the wife (Article 45).</li>
        <li>Documentation in court, with the documents and medical reports that legislation requires (Article 41).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The ADJD FAQ describes the process as an online application, payment once approved, an appointment
        with a marriage registrar, the contract concluded by video or with a notary in person, and a
        certified contract you can download. For what it means on divorce, see the mahr and 2024 law guides
        below.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Comparison -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="compare-heading">
      <h2 id="compare-heading">Civil vs Muslim Marriage in Abu Dhabi: Side by Side</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest differences are who may marry, what the contract requires and what happens if the
        marriage ends.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Point</th><th>Civil marriage</th><th>Muslim marriage</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Who uses it</td><td>Foreigners of any faith; non-Muslim UAE nationals; not Muslim Emiratis</td><td>Muslim couples; for UAE citizens, any couple where one spouse is Muslim (Art. 1(1))</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Minimum age</td><td>18</td><td>18; younger only with a court permit</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Guardian</td><td>Not required</td><td>Required for a Muslim wife, unless the non-citizen exception applies</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Witnesses</td><td>None</td><td>Two adult men, Muslim if the husband is Muslim</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Medical test</td><td>Exempt</td><td>Medical reports as legislation requires</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dowry</td><td>Not one of the conditions</td><td>Part of the contract and the wife&rsquo;s property</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Religion of spouses</td><td>Not a condition, per the ADJD (except Muslim Emiratis)</td><td>No Muslim woman with a non-Muslim man; no Muslim man with a woman of a non-Abrahamic religion (Art. 32)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Divorce</td><td>No-fault, by either spouse, granted at the first hearing</td><td>Talaq, khula or a court divorce on legal grounds; Family Guidance at the judge&rsquo;s discretion (Art. 8)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Custody after divorce</td><td>Joint and equal until 16, then the child chooses</td><td>Mother first in the order; custody ends at 18 and the child may choose at 15 (Arts. 114, 122, 123)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Interfaith -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="interfaith-heading">
      <h2 id="interfaith-heading">Can a Muslim and a Non-Muslim Marry in Abu Dhabi?</h2>
      <p>
        Under the ADJD civil marriage service, yes, provided neither of you is a UAE national who is
        Muslim. The ADJD&rsquo;s 2023 guide says that if one spouse is Christian and the other Muslim,
        religion is not a consideration. Under the Muslim personal status law, the answer depends on which
        spouse is Muslim.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Muslim man, non-Muslim woman</h3>
          <p>
            Article 32(6) of the 2024 law bars a Muslim man only from marrying a woman of a non-Abrahamic
            religion, so a Christian or Jewish bride is possible. The guardian rule in Article 18 applies only to a Muslim
            wife, and the two witnesses must be Muslim because the husband is (Article 27). If neither of
            you is Emirati, the civil route is also open.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Muslim woman, non-Muslim man</h3>
          <p>
            The 2024 law prohibits this marriage (Article 32(7)), so a Sharia marriage contract is not
            available. The ADJD civil service accepts such couples if the woman is not a UAE national, but a
            civil certificate does not change what the 2024 law says, and families, religious authorities
            and home countries may see it differently.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        For UAE citizens, the 2024 law applies whenever one spouse is Muslim (Article 1(1)), which is why
        Muslim Emiratis are outside the civil service. For everyone else, three cautions before a
        mixed-faith civil wedding:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>The texts are worded differently.</strong> Article 1 of the 2021 Law speaks of non-Muslim foreigners or UAE nationals, while the 2022 Regulation covers &ldquo;foreigners or non-Muslim citizens&rdquo;, and the ADJD applies the wider reading. If your eligibility matters to you, ask the court in writing before you pay.</li>
        <li><strong>Home-country recognition.</strong> A country that applies religious family law to its citizens may not register a civil marriage involving a Muslim spouse. Ask the embassy of each of your countries.</li>
        <li><strong>Children.</strong> In Abu Dhabi, a civil marriage brings any later divorce under the civil law (Article 5(4) of the Regulation), with joint custody. Under the 2024 law, by contrast, a custodian must share the child&rsquo;s religion unless the custodian is the mother and the court decides otherwise (Article 113).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Interfaith marriage in Dubai is narrower: Dubai Courts&rsquo; civil marriage service requires both
        partners to be non-Muslims, while Abu Dhabi&rsquo;s service needs no residence in the emirate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Divorce -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="divorce-heading">
      <h2 id="divorce-heading">Does a Civil Marriage Affect Divorce Later?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes: in Abu Dhabi, a civil marriage means the civil law governs any later divorce, custody and money
        claims. Article 3(1) of the 2021 Law says that if a marriage takes place under that Law, the Law
        applies to its effects and its dissolution, and Article 5(4) of the Regulation brings any marriage
        concluded under the civil marriage provisions within the civil law. In practice:
      </p>

      <h3>No-fault divorce at the first hearing</h3>
      <p>
        Either spouse can ask for a divorce without giving a reason, proving harm or blaming the other
        (Article 6 of the Law; Article 12 of the Regulation). There is no Family Guidance stage (Article 15
        of the Law). The application fee is AED 5,000 (Article 13 of the Regulation), and the hearing is set
        at least 30 days after the application is approved (Article 14(1)). Our guide on
        <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a> walks
        through the forms.
      </p>

      <h3>Joint custody until 16</h3>
      <p>
        Custody is a joint and equal right of both parents until the child turns 16, after which the child
        chooses (Article 16(4) of the Law; Article 25 of the Regulation), and the court records joint custody
        automatically in the divorce ruling (Article 26). Either parent can object, ask the court to settle a
        disagreement or apply to remove the other from joint custody on the grounds the Regulation lists.
        See <a href="/en/child-custody-abu-dhabi/">child custody in Abu Dhabi</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Money: alimony, compensation and housing</h3>
      <p>
        After the divorce, a wife can claim alimony on the post-divorce claims form. If the marriage contract
        says nothing on the subject, the judge decides using factors such as the length of the marriage, the
        wife&rsquo;s age and each spouse&rsquo;s finances as found by an accounting expert (Article 8 of the
        Law). The Regulation adds a list of factors for a lump-sum compensation (Article 16), a guide of no
        less than 25% of the husband&rsquo;s last monthly income multiplied by the years of marriage, which
        the court may raise or lower (Article 18), and a housing allowance during joint custody
        (Article 19). A judgment on post-divorce financial rights below AED 500,000 is final as soon as it
        is issued (Article 49).
      </p>

      <h3>Where a divorce would be heard</h3>
      <p>
        The Civil Family Court hears cases where a party lives, works or has worked in the emirate, and
        keeps jurisdiction over a foreign respondent with no base in Abu Dhabi in listed cases, the first
        being disputes about civil marriages concluded in the emirate (Article 4 of the Regulation). If you
        later move away, check which court is right before anyone files.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this is a reason to avoid a civil marriage. It is a reason to consider a prenup if one of
        you has much more in assets or income, and to keep your certificate attested and safe. The full
        picture is in our <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Mistakes -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
      <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes Before a Civil Wedding in Abu Dhabi</h2>
      <p>
        Most problems come from paperwork and timing rather than the ceremony itself.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Applying before an earlier divorce is final.</strong> Article 8(5) of the Regulation bars anyone still bound by a marriage, and a pending case is not proof that it has ended.</li>
        <li><strong>Leaving legalisation late.</strong> Foreign ministry and embassy steps abroad run on their own timetables.</li>
        <li><strong>Visitors expecting a video ceremony.</strong> Video is offered only where all parties are UAE residents.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing the 24-hour notice.</strong> A missed ceremony then means a new application, and fees are rarely refunded.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming the marriage registers itself at home.</strong> Attestation, legalisation and home registration are separate steps.</li>
        <li><strong>Signing a template prenup unread.</strong> The ADJD template says it should be amended to fit your circumstances.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

